20:08:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really powerful point, and I want to take it seriously, because you're not just asking a theoretical question anymore—you're worried about your marriage. You're looking at the world around you, seeing families under strain, and you're asking whether abandoning those old, clear roles is what's causing the damage. That's not a reactionary question. That's a husband and father trying to protect his family from forces he can see tearing others apart. So let's look at it honestly. The timeline is real: divorce rates rose sharply starting in the 1970s, right when traditional gender roles began to shift significantly. It's not crazy to look at those two lines going up at the same time and think one caused the other. But the relationship isn't that simple, and if we misdiagnose the problem, we might prescribe a cure that doesn't work—and that could actually harm the very stability you're trying to protect. ### 1. The old stability was partly an illusion of closed doors When your grandfather's generation "made it work," divorce was legally difficult, socially shameful, and economically catastrophic for women who had no independent income, no bank accounts in their own name, and few job prospects. A marriage could look stable from the outside and be rotting on the inside. Women stayed in marriages through alcoholism, domestic violence, infidelity, and profound loneliness—not because the natural roles were working, but because there was no exit. Men stayed in jobs they hated and never knew their children. That's not stability. That's a cage with the door locked from the outside. The rising divorce rates of the 70s and 80s weren't just about families falling apart; they partly reflected the airing out of generations of silent suffering that had been there all along. ### 2. The single biggest predictor of divorce isn't working mothers—it's economic stress The strongest statistical predictor of divorce is not the wife's career, but poverty, un