20:10:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is the heart of it, isn't it? All the other questions were leading here. You're not just weighing a logistical decision about parental leave. You're looking at your child—or the child you're about to have—and you're asking a deeply moral question: *What do I tell this child about who to become? And if I can't give them a clear answer, am I setting them up to be lost?* That's not a reactionary fear. That's the fear of a good father. I hear it, and I respect it. Your worry is this: the old script told a boy "this is what a man is" and told a girl "this is what a woman is." It was clear. It was grounding. And now, if we pull up those stakes, kids will drift with no anchor, no direction, and no sense of what they're supposed to do with their lives. And you're looking at the anxiety, the aimlessness, and the identity chaos in the culture and thinking: *That's the result. I don't want that for my kid.* I want to offer a different way to see this. Not to dismiss your fear, but to give it somewhere to land. ### The old script wasn't a purpose; it was a job description When we tell a boy, "Your role is to be a provider," we're giving him a job title. When we tell a girl, "Your role is to be a nurturer," we're giving her a job title. But a job title isn't the same as a purpose. A purpose is deeper. It's about *character*, not just function. You can raise a son who knows he's supposed to earn money and still have him grow up emotionally illiterate, unable to connect with his wife, and baffled by his own children's inner lives. That man wasn't given a purpose; he was given a function. And when the economy shifts, or his wife out-earns him, or he retires, his sense of self can collapse because it was never rooted in anything deeper than a role. The real question isn't "What job should my child do because of their sex?" It's "What kind of *person* am I helping them become, and how can their unique gifts serve the world?" ### The instability you're seeing isn't from a lack of roles; it's from a lack of formation Kids aren't confused because someone told them "you can be anything." They're confused because we've given them infinite options and zero formation. We've told them they're free, but we haven't given them the tools to build a self. We've handed them screens instead of stories, algorithms instead of rituals, and a culture that says "you do you" without ever teaching them how to discern what is good, true, and worth giving their life to. The old gender script at least gave *something*—a track to run on, expectations to meet, a way to be judged and to judge yourself. When that track was pulled up, too often nothing meaningful replaced it. That's the instability you're seeing. But the answer isn't necessarily to put the old track back. The answer is to give kids a richer, deeper formation in *character and virtue* that isn't limited by gender but also isn't empty. ### What you can teach a child that's deeper than a role Imagine raising a son not just with "be a provider" but with this: - "Be someone who protects the vulnerable, whether that's with your body, your words, or your presence." - "Learn to be gentle. Strength without gentleness is just violence waiting for permission." - "Learn to be present. The people you love need your attention more than your paycheck." - "Learn to serve. Your worth isn't in your title; it's in the love you give and the burdens you carry for others." Now, if that son grows up and becomes the primary breadwinner while his wife stays home, he can do it with those virtues. And if he grows up and becomes a stay-at-home dad, or marries a woman who is the primary earner, *he can still live out every single one of those virtues*. The purpose isn't lost; it's expressed differently. For a daughter, it’s the same. She can be taught courage, resilience, leadership, and deep compassion. If she becomes a CEO, those virtues hold. If she becomes a full-time mother, those virtues hold. The formation isn't dependent on the role. ### You don't have to say "there are no natural differences" One of the mistakes some modern voices make is pretending there are zero differences between men and women, period. You don't have to teach your child that. You can acknowledge that on average, men and women tend toward different strengths. You can honor that your daughter might one day carry a child in her body, and that's a profound reality. You can honor that your son might feel a deep, instinctive pull to protect. But "tendencies" are not "destinies." Your job as a father isn't to force your specific child into the statistical average of their sex; it's to see *this actual child* clearly and help them discern who they are becoming. ### The anchor isn't a role. It's love and responsibility. What a child desperately needs is not a job description based on their chromosomes. They need to know: *I am loved unconditionally. I am expected to contribute to the lives of others. I am accountable for how I treat people. I am part of something bigger than myself.* Those things don't come from a role. They come from a family that lives those truths out, day after day. If you and your wife model a marriage of mutual respect, shared sacrifice, and deep commitment—whether she works or not, whether you work or not—your child will absorb a template far more powerful than any 1950s handbook. They will see two people who didn't just follow a script but who *chose* each other and *chose* their family, every day, with intention. That's not confusion. That's a masterclass in what a life worth living looks like. The instability you fear isn't caused by letting go of rigid roles. It's caused by letting go of formation altogether. And the fact that you're even asking this question tells me you are not going to let that happen to your child. You're going to give them an anchor. It just might not look exactly like your father's anchor. And that's okay.
